1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,160
 Hey everyone, this is Ryan here, and welcome back to our Peridontic series.

2
00:00:06,160 --> 00:00:11,480
 This video is going to be about surgical therapy in periodontics.

3
00:00:11,480 --> 00:00:16,040
 So I apologize in advance as it's going to be a really long video, but it's a really

4
00:00:16,040 --> 00:00:21,440
 interesting topic, so I hope we can have a lot of fun learning about it.

5
00:00:21,440 --> 00:00:29,120
 So local therapy is designed to remove bacterial plaque and local factors that favor its accumulation.

6
00:00:29,120 --> 00:00:33,480
 Now we already talked about non-surgical therapy, and when that's not enough to resolve the

7
00:00:33,480 --> 00:00:39,440
 disease, often surgical therapy is then considered.

8
00:00:39,440 --> 00:00:44,840
 Since we're talking about surgical therapy, we need to talk about gingival flap design.

9
00:00:44,840 --> 00:00:52,760
 So gingival flaps are used for better access and visibility to the area of concern.

10
00:00:52,760 --> 00:00:57,240
 So I'm going to go through each of these concepts, and they're all important to know for the

11
00:00:57,240 --> 00:01:02,260
 board exam, and I'll go through them one at a time so we can really understand the concept

12
00:01:02,260 --> 00:01:03,760
 behind them.

13
00:01:03,760 --> 00:01:08,000
 So the first one is that the base of the flap should be wider than the top of the flap to

14
00:01:08,000 --> 00:01:11,280
 ensure adequate blood supply.

15
00:01:11,280 --> 00:01:15,560
 So in this first image here, that idea is illustrated.

16
00:01:15,560 --> 00:01:22,200
 You can see the top of the flap is up here, and since it was flapped up, this area was

17
00:01:22,200 --> 00:01:28,720
 by the teeth, you can tell that the scalloping matches where the gingival margins were, and

18
00:01:28,720 --> 00:01:33,600
 the base of the flap is still in contact with the bone.

19
00:01:33,600 --> 00:01:38,160
 So you can notice the base of the flap is wider than the top, and this ensures that the minimal

20
00:01:38,160 --> 00:01:43,560
 amount of vasculature is severed, and the top of the flap can remain well vascularized

21
00:01:43,560 --> 00:01:46,480
 and vital after surgery.

22
00:01:46,480 --> 00:01:51,240
 Here's an example where the base is narrower than the top, and that's not good because we're

23
00:01:51,240 --> 00:01:57,360
 severing some vasculature here and here that could supply the top corners, and you can get

24
00:01:57,360 --> 00:02:01,040
 tissue necrosis, just not a good result.

25
00:02:01,040 --> 00:02:07,920
 The next concept is that incisions should occur over intact bone, not over bony defects

26
00:02:07,920 --> 00:02:10,080
 or eminences.

27
00:02:10,080 --> 00:02:14,800
 So you want to avoid vertical incisions over bony prominences, like by the canines, the

28
00:02:14,800 --> 00:02:21,600
 uniform ridge, lingual of the mandible, and you also want to avoid areas of bony defects.

29
00:02:21,600 --> 00:02:25,160
 So we can see this second image illustrating that idea.

30
00:02:25,160 --> 00:02:29,240
 These incisions are on a nice sound intact bone.

31
00:02:29,240 --> 00:02:37,760
 You can notice this area of recession, a big bony defect here, and this is not a good flap

32
00:02:37,760 --> 00:02:41,240
 because the vertical incision is right by that defect.

33
00:02:41,240 --> 00:02:46,280
 Say for example, we had to surgically extract that tooth, you certainly want the flap margin

34
00:02:46,280 --> 00:02:54,960
 to be at least one or two teeth over from the area you're working on.

35
00:02:54,960 --> 00:03:01,600
 You want the corners of the flap to be rounded, not sharp, vertical releases, these vertical

36
00:03:01,600 --> 00:03:06,920
 incisions should occur at the line angles of their respective teeth.

37
00:03:06,920 --> 00:03:12,120
 So that's where the third image comes in, and you can also appreciate this in the previous

38
00:03:12,120 --> 00:03:18,680
 two, but the ideal area for the margin of the flap to contact the tooth is going to

39
00:03:18,680 --> 00:03:21,960
 be right at the line angle.

40
00:03:21,960 --> 00:03:28,360
 That's because if you did it in the middle of the papilla, you'd cause loss of that papilla,

41
00:03:28,360 --> 00:03:32,240
 and you'd have the formation of what are known as black triangles.

42
00:03:32,240 --> 00:03:40,480
 If the edge of the flap came into the mid-facial surface of the tooth, then that often would

43
00:03:40,480 --> 00:03:44,840
 cause noticeable gingival recession.

44
00:03:44,840 --> 00:03:49,680
 So you don't want to be mid-facial, you don't want to be mid-papilla, you want to be in-between

45
00:03:49,680 --> 00:03:52,640
 the two and right at the line angle.

46
00:03:52,640 --> 00:04:01,280
 So line one here illustrates being mid-papilla, line two here illustrates being mid-facial.

47
00:04:01,280 --> 00:04:06,720
 So you also want to avoid vital structures, you should be careful high up in the labial

48
00:04:06,720 --> 00:04:11,720
 maxilla, in the infra-orbital region, and in the lingual mandible, where you have thin

49
00:04:11,720 --> 00:04:21,400
 tissue, where the lingual artery is, so you want to avoid vital structures like that.

50
00:04:21,400 --> 00:04:26,360
 And like I've talked about in previous videos, it's really important what the patient is doing

51
00:04:26,360 --> 00:04:31,240
 at home to take care of their teeth, just as important as what you're doing in the clinic.

52
00:04:31,240 --> 00:04:32,920
 If not more important.

53
00:04:32,920 --> 00:04:38,880
 So like that, post-operative flat control is the most important procedure after periodontal

54
00:04:38,880 --> 00:04:42,400
 surgery.

55
00:04:42,400 --> 00:04:46,400
 So the next thing we have to talk about for flaps, we talked about flap design, now let's

56
00:04:46,400 --> 00:04:51,120
 talk about the two different types in terms of thickness.

57
00:04:51,120 --> 00:04:56,600
 So we have the split or partial thickness flap or mucosal flap, I included all the names

58
00:04:56,600 --> 00:04:58,600
 here just to be thorough.

59
00:04:58,600 --> 00:05:03,760
 And the full thickness are mucoperiosteal flap.

60
00:05:03,760 --> 00:05:08,360
 So this name is helpful to include because it clues you in to the actual layers that

61
00:05:08,360 --> 00:05:11,240
 are included in the flap.

62
00:05:11,240 --> 00:05:18,560
 So for the mucosal one, we have the gingiva or the mucosa and the submucosa.

63
00:05:18,560 --> 00:05:24,520
 So whether your gingiva or mucosa depends on where you are relative to the muco gingival

64
00:05:24,520 --> 00:05:25,600
 junction.

65
00:05:25,600 --> 00:05:32,240
 So both of these include the epithelium and the laminopropia and the submucosa has the

66
00:05:32,240 --> 00:05:36,480
 vessels and the underlying nerves, things like that.

67
00:05:36,480 --> 00:05:41,320
 So this type of flap is a little less aggressive and it's used for muco gingival surgery because

68
00:05:41,320 --> 00:05:45,200
 we don't need to expose the bone, that would be unnecessary.

69
00:05:45,200 --> 00:05:52,080
 The full thickness flap is called mucoperiosteal because it includes the periostium.

70
00:05:52,080 --> 00:05:59,720
 So the periostium is that thin, really dense, connective tissue layer that directly contacts

71
00:05:59,720 --> 00:06:02,280
 and covers bone.

72
00:06:02,280 --> 00:06:06,800
 So we have to remove that and include that as part of the flap when we need to contact

73
00:06:06,800 --> 00:06:09,800
 when we need to work on the actual bone.

74
00:06:09,800 --> 00:06:15,200
 So it's used for osteosurgery and periodontal regeneration to permit primary closure as

75
00:06:15,200 --> 00:06:17,800
 well as in apically repositioned flaps.

76
00:06:17,800 --> 00:06:22,880
 We'll talk about all that later on in the video, but I will say primary closure when

77
00:06:22,880 --> 00:06:28,800
 I say that I'm talking about the edges of the soft tissue when sutured back together,

78
00:06:28,800 --> 00:06:29,800
 touch each other.

79
00:06:29,800 --> 00:06:35,480
 In other words, there's no open wound left, that would be secondary closure.

80
00:06:35,480 --> 00:06:41,500
 So whenever alveolar bone is exposed, like in the full thickness flaps, expect about one

81
00:06:41,500 --> 00:06:44,400
 millimeter of bone resorption and remodeling.

82
00:06:44,400 --> 00:06:49,880
 This is sort of a natural body response and should expect some amount of resorption anytime

83
00:06:49,880 --> 00:06:53,600
 bone is exposed to the oral cavity.

84
00:06:53,600 --> 00:06:59,400
 So talked about flap design and now we talked about the two different types of partial thickness

85
00:06:59,400 --> 00:07:03,200
 and full thickness flaps.

86
00:07:03,200 --> 00:07:08,080
 Papilla preservation is a concept that I've sort of already talked about.

87
00:07:08,080 --> 00:07:12,040
 We talked about how we don't want to split the papilla, that was sort of the conventional

88
00:07:12,040 --> 00:07:16,600
 flap design was have the margins go straight through the papilla, but then we were getting

89
00:07:16,600 --> 00:07:21,360
 this papilla loss and formation of black triangles.

90
00:07:21,360 --> 00:07:27,720
 So the papilla preservation flap is to preserve the papilla.

91
00:07:27,720 --> 00:07:31,760
 So now this is sort of different from what we're talking about here.

92
00:07:31,760 --> 00:07:35,680
 These are wider flaps to expose a large area of bone.

93
00:07:35,680 --> 00:07:41,040
 These are smaller incisions if we just wanted to work on a smaller localized area, but you

94
00:07:41,040 --> 00:07:43,660
 can see the concept is the same.

95
00:07:43,660 --> 00:07:48,200
 The edges of the flap contact the line angles of the teeth.

96
00:07:48,200 --> 00:07:53,120
 So the three types of papilla preservation flaps are detailed in these three lines.

97
00:07:53,120 --> 00:07:57,880
 This one goes from the lingual line angle of this tooth to the facial line angle of this

98
00:07:57,880 --> 00:07:59,080
 tooth.

99
00:07:59,080 --> 00:08:03,120
 This one's scalloped from lingual line angle to lingual line angle.

100
00:08:03,120 --> 00:08:07,840
 This one's scalloped from facial line angle to facial line angle.

101
00:08:07,840 --> 00:08:12,360
 So those are papilla preservation flaps.

102
00:08:12,360 --> 00:08:16,160
 So let's talk a little bit more about the full thickness flap.

103
00:08:16,160 --> 00:08:21,320
 Since we're involving the periosteum layer, there are some specific incisions that we need

104
00:08:21,320 --> 00:08:24,960
 to know when doing this type of flap.

105
00:08:24,960 --> 00:08:33,720
 So the first one is the internal or reverse bevel incision, and that's shown as one in

106
00:08:33,720 --> 00:08:39,080
 this image, and it's about one millimeter from the gingival margin, the crest of the

107
00:08:39,080 --> 00:08:40,080
 gingiva.

108
00:08:40,080 --> 00:08:42,480
 So you can see it's a little bit apart from that.

109
00:08:42,480 --> 00:08:47,360
 It's beveled, it's not straight up and down, straight side to side, and it's used to remove

110
00:08:47,360 --> 00:08:52,640
 the pocket lining, yet conserve the outer gingiva.

111
00:08:52,640 --> 00:08:58,400
 The circular or curvicular incision shown as number two here is right through the base

112
00:08:58,400 --> 00:09:01,960
 of the pocket to the alveolar crest.

113
00:09:01,960 --> 00:09:06,840
 So you can imagine like sticking a periodontal probe into a pocket instead of now we're taking

114
00:09:06,840 --> 00:09:13,880
 a scalpel and cutting through the pocket to the bone.

115
00:09:13,880 --> 00:09:18,280
 And number three here is the interdental or interproximal incision.

116
00:09:18,280 --> 00:09:22,480
 And that's to remove the collar of tissue around the tooth you created with the first

117
00:09:22,480 --> 00:09:23,480
 two incisions.

118
00:09:23,480 --> 00:09:29,800
 So you can see number one contacts this area of bone, number two contacts this area of bone,

119
00:09:29,800 --> 00:09:34,680
 and so there's this little piece of soft tissue that's still connected to bone that we're

120
00:09:34,680 --> 00:09:40,120
 just going to slice off with that last third incision.

121
00:09:40,120 --> 00:09:45,040
 So the combination of all three of those incisions is what we could call a modified

122
00:09:45,040 --> 00:09:46,120
 Whitman flap.

123
00:09:46,120 --> 00:09:52,640
 It involves those three incisions and provides access to sub-gensurable areas for debridement,

124
00:09:52,640 --> 00:09:53,640
 for cleaning.

125
00:09:53,640 --> 00:09:59,400
 If there's calculus there for say we couldn't get with a conventional scaling and replaning.

126
00:09:59,400 --> 00:10:03,760
 And with the goal of new attachment.

127
00:10:03,760 --> 00:10:10,200
 Now we also have an apically repositioned flap which requires the addition of vertical

128
00:10:10,200 --> 00:10:12,120
 releasing incisions.

129
00:10:12,120 --> 00:10:17,280
 Note that all three of these were horizontal and involved the top of the flap here.

130
00:10:17,280 --> 00:10:22,840
 And now we're adding in these two vertical slices so that we can, and you want to make

131
00:10:22,840 --> 00:10:31,120
 sure they're beyond the mucogen jivel junction and so then you can safely, you can safely

132
00:10:31,120 --> 00:10:38,920
 get underneath the jingiva and the mucosa here, flap this area up, and you can position

133
00:10:38,920 --> 00:10:43,960
 the flap so that the top of the flap doesn't come up to where it was here.

134
00:10:43,960 --> 00:10:49,240
 It actually, you position it so it's a little bit apical to where it was originally.

135
00:10:49,240 --> 00:10:52,200
 And that's in order to attain pocket reduction.

136
00:10:52,200 --> 00:10:59,060
 By moving, physically moving the jingiva margin apically.

137
00:10:59,060 --> 00:11:05,780
 So the peridontal pack is a device that's sometimes used after this sort of invasive

138
00:11:05,780 --> 00:11:07,520
 flap surgery.

139
00:11:07,520 --> 00:11:11,800
 Usually consists of ZOE which is zinc oxide eugenol.

140
00:11:11,800 --> 00:11:17,760
 And you might recognize this if you watched my endodontics videos it's also the main ingredient

141
00:11:17,760 --> 00:11:21,920
 in gut-apertia and endodontic sealer.

142
00:11:21,920 --> 00:11:26,160
 They leave it in place typically for one week and they're placed to protect the surgical

143
00:11:26,160 --> 00:11:32,760
 wound, minimize discomfort, maintain tissue placement, and help prevent post-op bleeding.

144
00:11:32,760 --> 00:11:38,920
 The important thing to know for the board exam is that packs do not enhance healing.

145
00:11:38,920 --> 00:11:44,080
 You can think of the zinc oxide eugenol as having sort of a soothing quality so it doesn't

146
00:11:44,080 --> 00:11:48,800
 enhance healing but it does protect these things.

147
00:11:48,800 --> 00:11:52,220
 So you'd think it would enhance healing but it actually doesn't so that's an important

148
00:11:52,220 --> 00:11:54,920
 distinction to know for the board exam.

149
00:11:54,920 --> 00:11:56,920
 Little bit counterintuitive.

150
00:11:56,920 --> 00:12:03,480
 Alright, so the first category of surgery is gingival surgery.

151
00:12:03,480 --> 00:12:13,080
 So this is surgery only involving the jingiva, only soft tissue above the mucogenible junction.

152
00:12:13,080 --> 00:12:14,720
 There are two types of gingival surgery.

153
00:12:14,720 --> 00:12:21,040
 It's gingivectomy which is excision of gingiva to eliminate superboni pockets or gingival

154
00:12:21,040 --> 00:12:27,680
 enlargements say drug-induced gingival enlargement from a calcium channel blocker.

155
00:12:27,680 --> 00:12:34,880
 Gingivalplasty is excision of gingiva, sound similar, to reshape tissue deformities.

156
00:12:34,880 --> 00:12:39,560
 So you might be thinking what the heck is the difference between the two of those?

157
00:12:39,560 --> 00:12:41,880
 They sound awfully similar.

158
00:12:41,880 --> 00:12:46,840
 Now the difference between the two is the treatment objective.

159
00:12:46,840 --> 00:12:54,080
 So the ectomy is more aggressive and against to eliminate pockets or enlargements.

160
00:12:54,080 --> 00:12:58,800
 And the plasti is just some aesthetic reshaping.

161
00:12:58,800 --> 00:13:03,680
 It's more for the aesthetics, less aggressive re-contouring.

162
00:13:03,680 --> 00:13:11,040
 So it's the way that it's used and the objective that you're trying to achieve is where you

163
00:13:11,040 --> 00:13:12,960
 would distinguish between the two terms.

164
00:13:12,960 --> 00:13:15,640
 That's how I think of it anyway.

165
00:13:15,640 --> 00:13:21,600
 Healing by this type of surgery is by secondary intention only because there is no tissue

166
00:13:21,600 --> 00:13:23,320
 to approximate.

167
00:13:23,320 --> 00:13:28,400
 So I mentioned primary closure before, closure and intention, same thing.

168
00:13:28,400 --> 00:13:33,480
 Primary closure where the wound edges are approximated, they touch each other.

169
00:13:33,480 --> 00:13:36,960
 The secondary closure or intention is where they do not.

170
00:13:36,960 --> 00:13:38,400
 So you have an open wound.

171
00:13:38,400 --> 00:13:42,240
 And this makes sense because if you're just cutting ginger butt off, there's nothing you

172
00:13:42,240 --> 00:13:50,640
 can reattach, suture together, you're going to have some amount of open wound there.

173
00:13:50,640 --> 00:13:54,600
 A specific type of gingival surgery is distal wedge surgery.

174
00:13:54,600 --> 00:13:58,880
 This is for pocket reduction distal to terminal molars.

175
00:13:58,880 --> 00:14:03,240
 Depending if you're in the upper arch or lower arch, this looks different.

176
00:14:03,240 --> 00:14:08,600
 So for the maxillary terminal molar, you do a full thickness flap, so you're including

177
00:14:08,600 --> 00:14:12,800
 periosteum in that, with parallel incisions.

178
00:14:12,800 --> 00:14:16,240
 So the two incisions here are parallel to each other.

179
00:14:16,240 --> 00:14:24,480
 For mandibular, again, full thickness flap, including periosteum, with a V-shaped incisions.

180
00:14:24,480 --> 00:14:25,600
 So you can see that in this.

181
00:14:25,600 --> 00:14:31,400
 So I love these two images because they clearly denote the two different types of incisions

182
00:14:31,400 --> 00:14:36,280
 used, and believe it or not, this is definitely important to know for the board exam.

183
00:14:36,280 --> 00:14:41,400
 It's a little bit of a niche surgery, but it's certainly important to know.

184
00:14:41,400 --> 00:14:42,400
 All right.

185
00:14:42,400 --> 00:14:49,160
 The second category of surgery that we're going to talk about is mucogengival surgery.

186
00:14:49,160 --> 00:14:55,200
 So now we're including both the gingiva and the mucosa, all the soft tissue above and

187
00:14:55,200 --> 00:14:58,880
 below the mucogengival junction.

188
00:14:58,880 --> 00:15:07,480
 So I don't know if I can overemphasize how important this is to know.

189
00:15:07,480 --> 00:15:13,320
 Knowing the free gingiva graft and the connective tissue graft is going to get you so many questions

190
00:15:13,320 --> 00:15:15,880
 and it's just so great to know it.

191
00:15:15,880 --> 00:15:20,880
 It really cleared everything up for my head when I was in dental school.

192
00:15:20,880 --> 00:15:27,360
 So the free gingiva graft is, well, let me just say, these are the two main types of

193
00:15:27,360 --> 00:15:32,540
 soft tissue grafts, and definitely, again, commit this to memory, and I hope I can explain

194
00:15:32,540 --> 00:15:36,760
 it to you so you really understand the concepts here.

195
00:15:36,760 --> 00:15:42,520
 So caratinized tissue, as we talked about in the very first video in this series, includes

196
00:15:42,520 --> 00:15:47,400
 both the free gingiva and the attached gingiva.

197
00:15:47,400 --> 00:15:52,200
 And of these two, the attached gingiva is the most ideal tissue type, but both of these

198
00:15:52,200 --> 00:16:03,260
 are preferable to the non-caratinized alveolar mucosa, and that's because this caratin layer

199
00:16:03,260 --> 00:16:05,360
 is a lot stronger.

200
00:16:05,360 --> 00:16:10,840
 The whole purpose of this graft, the free gingiva graft, is that we really want caratinized

201
00:16:10,840 --> 00:16:16,480
 tissue to surround not only natural teeth, but also crowns and implants because it's

202
00:16:16,480 --> 00:16:17,480
 stronger.

203
00:16:17,480 --> 00:16:24,960
 Proper and less easy to irritate on tooth brushing because of the caratin layer.

204
00:16:24,960 --> 00:16:32,440
 So in this example here, we have a lot of soft tissue recession around an implant here

205
00:16:32,440 --> 00:16:35,200
 and you can see this darker mucosa.

206
00:16:35,200 --> 00:16:42,080
 The lighter areas are caratinized, that's because the caratin makes it look whiter, and

207
00:16:42,080 --> 00:16:45,880
 this area, we have mucosa right up against the implant.

208
00:16:45,880 --> 00:16:50,200
 And so that's going to get really irritated if the patient's trying to brush there.

209
00:16:50,200 --> 00:16:57,000
 It's not as resistant to the irritation as the caratinized layer is.

210
00:16:57,000 --> 00:17:02,800
 So the free gingiva graft is to widen the band of caratinized tissue.

211
00:17:02,800 --> 00:17:08,880
 So what we would do, if I can get the pen out, is we would want to perform a free gingiva

212
00:17:08,880 --> 00:17:16,400
 graft in this area here, and that could be a valid treatment option for the patient to

213
00:17:16,400 --> 00:17:26,000
 promote the growth of caratinized tissue there where there's only alveolar mucosa there now.

214
00:17:26,000 --> 00:17:32,800
 In contrast, the connective tissue graft involves taking a layer of connective tissue, usually

215
00:17:32,800 --> 00:17:38,440
 from the palate and sutured, over an exposed root surface.

216
00:17:38,440 --> 00:17:47,420
 So let's say this tooth right here has a lot of recession, and we could place a connective

217
00:17:47,420 --> 00:17:55,560
 tissue graft over this area of exposed root in order to promote the coverage of that root.

218
00:17:55,560 --> 00:18:00,160
 So say that patient was really sensitive in that area where there was exposed dentin,

219
00:18:00,160 --> 00:18:05,360
 and the connective tissue graft could be a great treatment option for that patient.

220
00:18:05,360 --> 00:18:10,800
 So what you do, again, is take this connective tissue from the palate, you'd suture it over

221
00:18:10,800 --> 00:18:16,360
 the exposed root surface, usually create a coronaly advanced flap to move the already

222
00:18:16,360 --> 00:18:22,400
 existing, the ideal caratinized tissue layer up and over this new graft.

223
00:18:22,400 --> 00:18:27,960
 And so this type of graft is to cover the exposed root surface.

224
00:18:27,960 --> 00:18:30,000
 So there's a big difference between the two.

225
00:18:30,000 --> 00:18:36,220
 They may seem slightly similar, and they are, but in a very simplified but helpful way of

226
00:18:36,220 --> 00:18:38,280
 differentiating the two.

227
00:18:38,280 --> 00:18:46,000
 The free gingival graft happens below or apical to the gingival margin, and the connective

228
00:18:46,000 --> 00:18:52,320
 tissue graft happens above or coronal to the gingival margin.

229
00:18:52,320 --> 00:18:58,120
 So one is to widen the band of caratinized tissue, and the other one is for root coverage.

230
00:18:58,120 --> 00:19:03,800
 So knowing and distinguishing between these two grafts is super, super important, and

231
00:19:03,800 --> 00:19:08,120
 I promise will really, really help.

232
00:19:08,120 --> 00:19:13,120
 The last three things to mention here for mucogen gingival surgery, we can do a phrenectomy,

233
00:19:13,120 --> 00:19:19,120
 which is a complete removal of the phrenom, phrenotomy is just an incision of the phrenom,

234
00:19:19,120 --> 00:19:20,600
 not a complete removal.

235
00:19:20,600 --> 00:19:25,640
 So again, we see theectomy being the more aggressive form of surgery, and we'll see that

236
00:19:25,640 --> 00:19:31,520
 again in the future, and vestibule plastic, where you make an incision in the base of

237
00:19:31,520 --> 00:19:35,080
 the vestibule in order to deepen it.

238
00:19:35,080 --> 00:19:41,280
 All right, and this is sort of a review of what we just talked about if you want to go

239
00:19:41,280 --> 00:19:42,880
 over it in writing.

240
00:19:42,880 --> 00:19:47,960
 So this includes some measurements, and if you're interested, why it's called a free gingival

241
00:19:47,960 --> 00:19:56,120
 graft, free by definition, a free graft by definition is transplanted without a nourishing

242
00:19:56,120 --> 00:19:57,240
 blood supply.

243
00:19:57,240 --> 00:20:03,600
 So it must undergo revascularization from the recipient bed that's receiving that graft.

244
00:20:03,600 --> 00:20:09,200
 So the free gingival graft is only the epithelium layer, it lacks a blood supply.

245
00:20:09,200 --> 00:20:15,000
 So when you perform a split thickness flap, you expose the vascular bed at the recipient

246
00:20:15,000 --> 00:20:21,520
 site so that I can feed and nourish the new caratinized tissue.

247
00:20:21,520 --> 00:20:25,440
 And connective tissue graft, same sort of thing, this is just a review about what we

248
00:20:25,440 --> 00:20:30,720
 talked about, and I briefly mentioned this, but the palate is the most common donor site

249
00:20:30,720 --> 00:20:35,280
 for both of these, for both the free gingival graft and the connective tissue graft.

250
00:20:35,280 --> 00:20:45,200
 So definitely remember that the palate is the most often used site for this sort of surgery.

251
00:20:45,200 --> 00:20:49,760
 All right, so the third category of surgery is osteosurgery.

252
00:20:49,760 --> 00:20:56,000
 So now we're no longer talking about soft tissue, now we're talking about the bone.

253
00:20:56,000 --> 00:21:01,640
 And just like anything, before we do any treatment, we want to observe and analyze what's there

254
00:21:01,640 --> 00:21:02,880
 to start with.

255
00:21:02,880 --> 00:21:09,080
 So let's talk about bone architecture and what this concept refers to.

256
00:21:09,080 --> 00:21:15,160
 So visualization of bony architecture allows the clinician to determine the types of bony

257
00:21:15,160 --> 00:21:19,720
 defects that are present and the extent of those defects.

258
00:21:19,720 --> 00:21:27,120
 Positive architecture means that the interproximal bone is coronal to radicular bone, and that's

259
00:21:27,120 --> 00:21:30,160
 what we see in image A.

260
00:21:30,160 --> 00:21:37,480
 So the interproximal bone that is between the teeth is higher than the radicular bone, which

261
00:21:37,480 --> 00:21:42,640
 is around the root, and that's pretty normal.

262
00:21:42,640 --> 00:21:46,560
 Flat architecture is where interproximal and radicular bone are at the same height.

263
00:21:46,560 --> 00:21:50,640
 It's flat across.

264
00:21:50,640 --> 00:21:57,080
 Negative architecture is where the interproximal bone is apical to the radicular bone, and that's

265
00:21:57,080 --> 00:22:01,640
 the opposite of what is normal healthy bone architecture.

266
00:22:01,640 --> 00:22:09,880
 So positive architecture is what we want.

267
00:22:09,880 --> 00:22:13,760
 So for osteosurgery, we have two different types of surgery.

268
00:22:13,760 --> 00:22:19,640
 We have the ostectomy, which is removal of supporting bone, and osteotomy, which is

269
00:22:19,640 --> 00:22:22,520
 removal of non-supporting bone.

270
00:22:22,520 --> 00:22:28,760
 To remember, gingivectomy was excision of tissue and gingivoplasty was this more conservative

271
00:22:28,760 --> 00:22:31,600
 reshaping and recontoring.

272
00:22:31,600 --> 00:22:37,920
 So again, theectomy is the more aggressive type, same situation here.

273
00:22:37,920 --> 00:22:44,160
 Ostectomy is again removal of supporting alveolar bone, meaning it's directly supporting the

274
00:22:44,160 --> 00:22:50,120
 tooth, it's indirect contact with the periodontal ligament.

275
00:22:50,120 --> 00:22:55,400
 Theectomy, in contrast, is reduction of bone away from the tooth.

276
00:22:55,400 --> 00:23:02,480
 They both aim to create a more physiologic, positive bone architecture, but one does with

277
00:23:02,480 --> 00:23:06,200
 removal of supporting bone, and one does not.

278
00:23:06,200 --> 00:23:10,560
 So that's the main distinction between those two.

279
00:23:10,560 --> 00:23:15,560
 After ostectomy, there's often these peaks of bone remaining at the line angles called

280
00:23:15,560 --> 00:23:21,040
 widow's peaks, which predispose to periodontal pockets in these areas, basically saying to

281
00:23:21,040 --> 00:23:24,480
 do a thorough job, smooth everything, round everything out.

282
00:23:24,480 --> 00:23:28,960
 So you don't have these sharp areas of bone left over, but sometimes you can't help it

283
00:23:28,960 --> 00:23:37,120
 and have to go back and do an osteotomy to reshape and recontour that area.

284
00:23:37,120 --> 00:23:43,360
 So I also want to briefly tie in an important concept called clinical crown lengthening.

285
00:23:43,360 --> 00:23:49,200
 This is where you want to expose more to structure by lowering the bone for several different

286
00:23:49,200 --> 00:23:54,160
 purposes, one of which is gaining more feral for crown support, which I have a separate

287
00:23:54,160 --> 00:23:58,600
 video on the feral effect and biologic width, so check that out if you want to learn more

288
00:23:58,600 --> 00:24:00,880
 about those concepts.

289
00:24:00,880 --> 00:24:06,880
 But clinical crown lengthening is essentially combining osteous resection and ostectomy,

290
00:24:06,880 --> 00:24:16,080
 lowering the bone with either a gingivectomy by cutting the collar of gingiva away.

291
00:24:16,080 --> 00:24:21,440
 And you would do that if there would be at least two millimeters of keratinized tissue

292
00:24:21,440 --> 00:24:27,200
 remaining after the gingivectomy, or otherwise, you'd have to do an apically positioned flap,

293
00:24:27,200 --> 00:24:32,520
 like I talked about before, physically moving the gingival margin down.

294
00:24:32,520 --> 00:24:36,360
 And that would be to conserve the keratinized tissue that is there.

295
00:24:36,360 --> 00:24:43,520
 So osteosurgery is used in a whole bunch of areas in dentistry, in periodontics, and in

296
00:24:43,520 --> 00:24:46,400
 prosthodontics to get more feral effects.

297
00:24:46,400 --> 00:24:54,540
 So you can see how removing some bone, lowering the bone area will also effectively lower where

298
00:24:54,540 --> 00:25:02,280
 the gingiva is if you combine it with a gingivectomy or apically positioned flap.

299
00:25:02,280 --> 00:25:08,440
 So mechanisms of healing after surgery, there are a couple of different ways that the situation

300
00:25:08,440 --> 00:25:09,640
 can go.

301
00:25:09,640 --> 00:25:16,360
 You can have regeneration, which is a complete restoration of architecture and function,

302
00:25:16,360 --> 00:25:21,160
 or repair, which is not completely restoring architecture and function.

303
00:25:21,160 --> 00:25:28,760
 It involves healing by scar or formation of long, junctional epithelium.

304
00:25:28,760 --> 00:25:33,880
 So we talked about junctional epithelium, it has a weak hemidesma zone while attachment

305
00:25:33,880 --> 00:25:38,600
 to enamel, but it is a sign of healing towards a shallower pocket.

306
00:25:38,600 --> 00:25:45,120
 This term long, junctional epithelium is used when a pocket was once very deep, and since

307
00:25:45,120 --> 00:25:51,640
 the bone and periodontal ligament have been irreversibly damaged and gone, this had this

308
00:25:51,640 --> 00:26:01,720
 physically long pocket that was formed, the soft tissue that comes into its place is a

309
00:26:01,720 --> 00:26:07,960
 very physically long, several millimeters long worth of junctional epithelium that attaches

310
00:26:07,960 --> 00:26:14,200
 to the tooth, and that's the best we can hope for natural healing.

311
00:26:14,200 --> 00:26:21,240
 And so these two terms are formid dichotomy, and these two formid dichotomy as well.

312
00:26:21,240 --> 00:26:27,240
 Reattachment is the reunion of epithelial and connective tissue with the tooth root surface

313
00:26:27,240 --> 00:26:34,200
 after incision or injury, whereas new attachment is embedding of fresh new PDL fibers into new

314
00:26:34,200 --> 00:26:39,480
 cement that has been previously deprived of its original attachment.

315
00:26:39,480 --> 00:26:45,720
 So you may be wondering, well, Ryan, you just talked about how bone and PDL were irreversibly

316
00:26:45,720 --> 00:26:50,040
 damaged and the best we can hope for is this long, junctional epithelium attached into

317
00:26:50,040 --> 00:26:54,360
 the tooth, but how do we get new attachment with new PDL?

318
00:26:54,360 --> 00:26:56,880
 Well, wonder no longer.

319
00:26:56,880 --> 00:27:04,040
 The last category of surgery is regenerative surgery, and this is where we seek to actually

320
00:27:04,040 --> 00:27:07,960
 regenerate tissue that was lost.

321
00:27:07,960 --> 00:27:15,520
 So periodontal regeneration, also formerly known as guided tissue regeneration, where

322
00:27:15,520 --> 00:27:20,480
 we want to regenerate bone, cementum, and PDL.

323
00:27:20,480 --> 00:27:27,080
 Yes, we're going to try to reverse and regenerate bone and PDL and cementum that was lost by

324
00:27:27,080 --> 00:27:29,640
 the periodontal disease.

325
00:27:29,640 --> 00:27:31,680
 Now this can't be done in every situation.

326
00:27:31,680 --> 00:27:39,320
 There's very specific situations and really good patient motivation and oral hygiene to

327
00:27:39,320 --> 00:27:45,600
 make this stuff happen, but it is possible in select situations.

328
00:27:45,600 --> 00:27:52,200
 So in this treatment, there are three B's that are free-winly used by operators to

329
00:27:52,200 --> 00:27:57,480
 have the best chance for successful tissue regeneration.

330
00:27:57,480 --> 00:28:03,520
 So I'm a big fan of video games, and there are three classic roles that a group of players

331
00:28:03,520 --> 00:28:07,720
 working together would assume to take on a big boss.

332
00:28:07,720 --> 00:28:13,440
 So I'm going to diverge for a second here and talk about these three roles.

333
00:28:13,440 --> 00:28:18,920
 And I think they'll help you remember these three B's.

334
00:28:18,920 --> 00:28:24,840
 So the tank takes the damage from the boss and protects the rest of the players.

335
00:28:24,840 --> 00:28:31,160
 The damage focused on dealing damage and defeating the boss, and the healers are responsible

336
00:28:31,160 --> 00:28:34,400
 for making sure the other players stay alive.

337
00:28:34,400 --> 00:28:40,360
 So I remember the three materials of the regeneration process by assigning each one of these roles

338
00:28:40,360 --> 00:28:43,320
 to each one of these materials.

339
00:28:43,320 --> 00:28:46,800
 So the barrier membrane is like the tank.

340
00:28:46,800 --> 00:28:53,600
 It's placed over the defect and its job is to protect the area.

341
00:28:53,600 --> 00:28:58,200
 The bone graft is the damage because it's doing the bulk of the work.

342
00:28:58,200 --> 00:29:04,080
 It gets right in there and it's used to regenerate the missing bone.

343
00:29:04,080 --> 00:29:09,800
 And the biologic agent is like the healer because it's a medicament that creates a supportive

344
00:29:09,800 --> 00:29:14,440
 environment for the rest of the components to do their job.

345
00:29:14,440 --> 00:29:19,560
 So I remember the tank, the damage, and the healer as the three B's barrier membrane,

346
00:29:19,560 --> 00:29:21,920
 bone graft, and biologic agent.

347
00:29:21,920 --> 00:29:27,240
 So a little bit silly, but I think it really helps me remember what each of those things

348
00:29:27,240 --> 00:29:31,600
 are doing and let alone what they are.

349
00:29:31,600 --> 00:29:35,180
 So why do we need these three components for regeneration?

350
00:29:35,180 --> 00:29:39,280
 Well if we did nothing, this is what wound healing would look like.

351
00:29:39,280 --> 00:29:45,080
 And these cells populate a wound area during the healing process, listed from fastest to

352
00:29:45,080 --> 00:29:47,360
 slowest.

353
00:29:47,360 --> 00:29:53,220
 So the epithelial cells and the connective tissue cells would repopulate that wound area

354
00:29:53,220 --> 00:30:03,040
 the fastest and the PDL cells labeled four, bone cells labeled three are the two slowest.

355
00:30:03,040 --> 00:30:07,680
 So this should make sense because like I was talking about before, the natural healing

356
00:30:07,680 --> 00:30:13,960
 process would result in a long junctional epithelium and you'd get all this soft tissue

357
00:30:13,960 --> 00:30:22,760
 would grow down into this wound and the bone and PDL wouldn't get a chance to grow up and

358
00:30:22,760 --> 00:30:26,760
 restore that attachment that was once there.

359
00:30:26,760 --> 00:30:32,440
 So if we do nothing, let the wound heal, often get a long junctional epithelium, which is

360
00:30:32,440 --> 00:30:42,640
 a sign of healing, yes, but it's not a complete regeneration that would just be a repair.

361
00:30:42,640 --> 00:30:48,080
 So we really want to get a full regeneration when we're talking about periodontal regeneration

362
00:30:48,080 --> 00:30:51,640
 or guided tissue regeneration.

363
00:30:51,640 --> 00:30:56,280
 So that's why we use those three components.

364
00:30:56,280 --> 00:31:02,680
 The barrier membrane would slip in here and that would prevent the downgrowth of the soft

365
00:31:02,680 --> 00:31:12,600
 tissue components and we'd place the graft in here to provide a physical scaffolding to

366
00:31:12,600 --> 00:31:21,080
 promote new bone formation and we'd also use the biologic agent to help support everything.

367
00:31:21,080 --> 00:31:28,120
 So the tank, damage, and the heal.

368
00:31:28,120 --> 00:31:33,120
 You can also do direct root surface treatment with chelating agents like EDTA and citric

369
00:31:33,120 --> 00:31:40,720
 acid can expose collagen fibrils through de-mineralization and may improve the success

370
00:31:40,720 --> 00:31:43,120
 of new attachment.

371
00:31:43,120 --> 00:31:49,120
 Alright, so bone graft materials have a lot of definitions associated with them.

372
00:31:49,120 --> 00:31:55,800
 So I made them as simple as possible so we can remember them for the board exam.

373
00:31:55,800 --> 00:32:01,680
 So the best, well let's talk about these definitions first and then I can go a little bit more

374
00:32:01,680 --> 00:32:02,680
 in depth.

375
00:32:02,680 --> 00:32:05,720
 So we have four different types of bone graft materials.

376
00:32:05,720 --> 00:32:10,640
 There's autographed, which means the bone comes from yourself.

377
00:32:10,640 --> 00:32:15,280
 Allograft is from another human, usually a cadaver.

378
00:32:15,280 --> 00:32:23,780
 The xenograft is from another animal, usually a cow, and the aloplast is a synthetic or

379
00:32:23,780 --> 00:32:24,780
 inorganic.

380
00:32:24,780 --> 00:32:32,960
 You can think of like plastic being a synthetic material, just like aloplast is a synthetic

381
00:32:32,960 --> 00:32:34,520
 material.

382
00:32:34,520 --> 00:32:41,400
 So those are the four main categories of graft materials and there are three main ideas that

383
00:32:41,400 --> 00:32:43,400
 grafts can carry out.

384
00:32:43,400 --> 00:32:50,600
 So osteoconductive means that the graft is forming a physical scaffold to hold that

385
00:32:50,600 --> 00:32:54,880
 area open and provide area for a bone to fill in.

386
00:32:54,880 --> 00:33:01,800
 Osteoinductive, I mean it's converting neighboring progenitor cells, these kind of young bone

387
00:33:01,800 --> 00:33:07,920
 cells that are nearby into osteoblasts that are going to in turn form bone.

388
00:33:07,920 --> 00:33:15,720
 Ogenic means that the bone graft material itself is actually forming new bone.

389
00:33:15,720 --> 00:33:23,400
 So the autographed is the best grafting material because it has all three of these qualities.

390
00:33:23,400 --> 00:33:27,040
 The autographed is conductive, inductive, and genic.

391
00:33:27,040 --> 00:33:28,920
 The allograft is the next best one.

392
00:33:28,920 --> 00:33:35,200
 It has the osteoconductive and inductive qualities, but not the genic.

393
00:33:35,200 --> 00:33:40,200
 And all of the bone grafts are osteoconductive.

394
00:33:40,200 --> 00:33:45,840
 So the xenograft and aloplast are osteoconductive but not the other two.

395
00:33:45,840 --> 00:33:57,400
 So listed from first to fourth is sort of the order from best to worst bone graft materials.

396
00:33:57,400 --> 00:34:01,240
 So a summary of everything we've talked about.

397
00:34:01,240 --> 00:34:06,920
 I know it's a lot, we've talked about the four categories of periodontal surgery, the

398
00:34:06,920 --> 00:34:10,640
 gingival, mucogengival, osteous, and regenerative.

399
00:34:10,640 --> 00:34:15,880
 And they kind of fit into these two overarching categories of additive where we're trying

400
00:34:15,880 --> 00:34:23,220
 to restore tissue, you can do that with regeneration, free gingival graft, connective tissue graft,

401
00:34:23,220 --> 00:34:28,920
 and advancing the flap cronally, or we can talk about a subtractive method where we're

402
00:34:28,920 --> 00:34:35,720
 trying to bring levels down and reduce pocket depths by doing osteosurgery, reconquering

403
00:34:35,720 --> 00:34:42,600
 to get that positive bony architecture, gingivectomy, or apically positioned flap to physically move

404
00:34:42,600 --> 00:34:48,720
 the gingival margin down and reduce pocket depths.

405
00:34:48,720 --> 00:34:53,560
 And tying it all together, I'm going to leave you with this slide full of beautiful things

406
00:34:53,560 --> 00:34:55,640
 to know for the board exam.

407
00:34:55,640 --> 00:35:01,640
 These make for really good board exam questions because it ties in the diagnosis classification

408
00:35:01,640 --> 00:35:06,440
 stuff we talked about from earlier in the series with the available treatment options

409
00:35:06,440 --> 00:35:09,800
 covered in this video.

410
00:35:09,800 --> 00:35:15,920
 So for, and this is maybe a bit of an overgeneralization, but definitely a great tool for the board

411
00:35:15,920 --> 00:35:16,920
 exam.

412
00:35:16,920 --> 00:35:24,520
 So one in two wall defects, you typically would opt to do osteous resection.

413
00:35:24,520 --> 00:35:33,560
 Also doing ostectomy to re-contour the bone to restore that ideal, natural, positive architecture.

414
00:35:33,560 --> 00:35:40,560
 Whereas if you have three and four wall defects, you would opt to try regeneration.

415
00:35:40,560 --> 00:35:47,200
 And that's because the more walls you have, the better blood supply and cell source proximity

416
00:35:47,200 --> 00:35:49,400
 you have.

417
00:35:49,400 --> 00:35:58,080
 So the deep narrow three wall defect is the ideal, theoretical ideal for regenerating

418
00:35:58,080 --> 00:36:00,480
 in for bony defects.

419
00:36:00,480 --> 00:36:07,440
 So a deep narrow three wall defect has the most surface area possible of available bone

420
00:36:07,440 --> 00:36:13,320
 to populate the graft with bone cells and also to protect the graft while everything

421
00:36:13,320 --> 00:36:16,280
 is regenerating.

422
00:36:16,280 --> 00:36:21,960
 So think about if you were in a big hurricane, would you rather be in a house with four

423
00:36:21,960 --> 00:36:24,760
 walls or a house with one wall?

424
00:36:24,760 --> 00:36:30,560
 And so the more walls you have, the more protected the graft is and the more walls there are

425
00:36:30,560 --> 00:36:36,000
 to provide new natural bone cells to form new bone in that area.

426
00:36:36,000 --> 00:36:38,440
 The same concept with forcations.

427
00:36:38,440 --> 00:36:46,280
 So a HAMP class II forcation and either the buckle of an upper molar or either buckle or

428
00:36:46,280 --> 00:36:52,280
 lingual of lower molars is ideal for regenerating forcation defects.

429
00:36:52,280 --> 00:37:00,160
 Again, the class II provides the deepest narrowest defect with the most available bony walls.

430
00:37:00,160 --> 00:37:06,560
 And the Miller class I with a thick gingival biotype, really nice thick gums and a wide

431
00:37:06,560 --> 00:37:13,920
 band of kratnized tissue at least two millimeters wide is ideal for regenerating recession defects.

432
00:37:13,920 --> 00:37:19,720
 The near, and we talked about this in the first video, but the near 100% chance of success

433
00:37:19,720 --> 00:37:28,000
 of root coverage with a connective tissue graft with this type of soft tissue defect.

434
00:37:28,000 --> 00:37:36,000
 So to remember, the best types of the ideal defects to restore with their respective treatments

435
00:37:36,000 --> 00:37:44,880
 are remember, three for three wall, two for HAMP class II, and one for Miller class I.

436
00:37:44,880 --> 00:37:49,640
 So tying it all together is as easy as three, two, and one.

437
00:37:49,640 --> 00:37:56,760
 Alright guys, I know that was a lot of material in this video, just go ahead and watch it

438
00:37:56,760 --> 00:38:01,840
 a few times and feel free to ask me questions in the comments if anything wasn't clear.

439
00:38:01,840 --> 00:38:05,960
 I really hope you enjoyed the video and thanks so much for watching everyone and thanks for

